1
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:07,800
The reason why it's there
is I will protect you

2
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:16,280
Don't worry, let's go ahead
The moon will come to the moon

3
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:23,320
The moon will come to the
moon Let's look at the moon

4
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:56,060
Fire! Emergency!

5
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:58,660
Everybody wake up! Damn them!

6
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:01,337
Those pirates started this, didn't they?

7
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:02,997
We need to do something quick!

8
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:04,777
There's still a lot of people left inside!

9
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:06,317
Iceberg might be trapped!

10
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:07,957
Let's make sure he made it out!

11
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:09,240
Right! Come on!

12
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:10,680
The rest of you!

13
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,140
Put out that fire! Go! Go!

14
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:23,940
It appears the fire is
beginning to spread.

15
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:31,540
Leopard, human, whatever the hell you are,

16
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:35,351
you're not going to lay one
finger on Iceberg, you hear me?

17
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:39,100
No, he's a carnivore Snow type.

18
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:41,418
They're the most brutal of them all.

19
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:42,690
Indeed.

20
00:02:42,773 --> 00:02:43,860
Logia-types,

21
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:50,020
Zoan-types, Paramethia-types, each one
extremely powerful in their own unique way.

22
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,197
The ability to enhance
one's own personal strength

23
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:55,917
happens to be the
specialty of the Zoan-type.

24
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,780
In other words, the more we
train, the stronger we get.

25
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:05,360
Which makes the Zoan-type the most lethal
when it comes to attacks at close range.

26
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,040
Iceberg! Go! Move it!

27
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:13,820
Luchi, the workmen are making their
way up the stairs. Don't worry.

28
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,944
They'll never make it this far.

29
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:17,420
Tempest kick!

30
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:30,260
Go back!

31
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:31,911
The ceiling's collapsing!

32
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:43,220
Nami, Dada!

33
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:52,470
Chopper!

34
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:54,760
What are you doing?

35
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:57,417
No matter what, I'm
getting you out of here.

36
00:03:57,500 --> 00:03:59,960
There's no way your wounds are far too...

37
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,640
I'm afraid we can't let you do that.

38
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:11,620
And I'm afraid, you're gonna have to.

39
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:16,600
Damn you!

40
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:20,180
Why did it have to be you guys?

41
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:25,260
No, I can't believe you!

42
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,120
How could you do this?

43
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:29,380
You tricked me!

44
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,365
I thought you were my friends!

45
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,160
Then you are mistaken.

46
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,620
Talking pigeon jerk!

47
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:53,400
Luffy, what are you?

48
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:00,980
Finger pistol.

49
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:03,910
Luffy!

50
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:30,200
Have a nice flight.

51
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:32,400
Noooooooo!

52
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:37,180
Kalifa!

53
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:41,730
Damn you!

54
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:43,820
Iron body!

55
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,960
Now it's your turn.

56
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:30,280
Did everyone make it out?

57
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:31,737
It's taken the building.

58
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:33,232
We can't go back inside.

59
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:35,717
Have each team check to
see if anyone's missing.

60
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,917
Hey, have you heard
anything about Iceberg?

61
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:38,830
Is he safe?

62
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:40,380
Don't tell me.

63
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:42,057
He's not stuck in there, is he?

64
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:42,997
Of course not.

65
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:44,517
The foremen are with him!

66
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:46,077
There's no way they left him behind!

67
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,101
Search the area!

68
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:49,006
I'm sure he's here somewhere!

69
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:54,780
Hey, wait.

70
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,157
That woman's one of the Straw Hats.

71
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:58,240
I'm sure of it.

72
00:06:59,140 --> 00:07:01,552
Did she jump out from one of the windows?

73
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:05,997
She probably got trapped by the fire and
didn't have any other choice in the matter.

74
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:08,220
Well, what should we do with her?

75
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:10,897
These damn pirates have
done a terrible thing here.

76
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:13,857
Let's make her tell us where
the rest of her crew is.

77
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:16,999
We'll hunt them down and
turn them over to the Navy.

78
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:23,520
The testimony of a pirate
won't hold up as evidence.

79
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:26,020
This case will remain a mystery.

80
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:32,500
Iceberg, no matter how great of
a shipbuilding engineer you are,

81
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:35,220
no matter how great a mayor,

82
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:40,000
a mere individual citizen shouldn't
oppose the world government.

83
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:54,437
Let's go we need to find tom's
other apprentice Franky or

84
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:58,180
rather cutty flab and take
those blueprints away from him?

85
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:14,280
Granny! Granny!

86
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:15,860
There's a huge fire!

87
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:18,740
It's Galilaw's main office.

88
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:20,640
Such a terrible thing to do.

89
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:24,251
What do you mean, Granny?

90
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:26,477
Did someone start the fire on purpose?

91
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,560
Well, I can't really say for sure.

92
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:32,920
But usually, when I get a bad
feeling, it turns out to be right.

93
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:35,680
I sure hope that Iceberg is doing okay.

94
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:43,200
Seriously, all this is happening over
that thing, it shouldn't even exist.

95
00:08:56,580 --> 00:09:00,517
You're sure of it, you saw Franky
wandering into your bar earlier?

96
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:04,517
Yeah, I spoke with him, but he
took off to go look for Straw Hat.

97
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:05,440
I see.

98
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:10,536
Seems like he doesn't stay
in one place for very long.

99
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:12,550
Wow.

100
00:09:12,633 --> 00:09:15,424
Some people just have the
worst sense of timing.

101
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:17,580
Look, there.

102
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:20,200
Listen up, Straw Hat!

103
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:23,520
We've got Long Nose! That's right!

104
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:24,980
We totally have him!

105
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:28,760
If you don't want him to sing
in the key of C! You idiot!

106
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,777
How many times do I have to tell you it's

107
00:09:30,860 --> 00:09:32,737
if you don't want him
to sink into the sea?

108
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:33,937
Break some iced tea!

109
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:35,480
The sea! Are you deaf?

110
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:36,677
What kind of threat is that?

111
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:38,017
It doesn't make any sense.

112
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:38,930
Whatever!

113
00:09:39,013 --> 00:09:40,897
All that matters is getting
the message to Straw Hat!

114
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:43,237
Giving him that stupid
message won't do any good.

115
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:45,237
And, bro, Franky will
end up kicking your butt.

116
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:47,217
If you don't want him sunk
to the bottom of the sea,

117
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:50,537
go to the warehouse under the
bridge with the guards, Franky!

118
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:51,450
Like that!

119
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:54,697
If he gets the message and he does come,

120
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:57,368
bro's gonna beat the
living crap out of him.

121
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:01,597
Who did you say is waiting for whom?

122
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:03,937
And where is this certain someone waiting?

123
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:04,850
Huh?

124
00:10:04,933 --> 00:10:05,855
Who the hell are you?

125
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:07,597
We're kind of busy right now.

126
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:08,510
Get lost!

127
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:12,894
If you want to live, you'll answer my
question in the next three seconds.

128
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:14,170
Huh?

129
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:17,760
Is that some kind of joke, you nutjob?

130
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:22,098
Do you even have the slightest clue
as to what family we're a part of?

131
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:27,039
What did you mean by the
warehouse under the bridge?

132
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:29,360
Is that the place where Franky is?

133
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:33,937
Whatever business you have with Franky?

134
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:37,400
He's gonna have to go
through us first, right guys?

135
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:02,400
Why in the world are the three
of you crying about it? What?

136
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:04,327
We're not crying, you idiot!

137
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:06,477
I was just thinking
about everything you've

138
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:08,497
been through to protect
that ship all alone,

139
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:09,992
and it touched my heart!

140
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:11,140
We're not crying!

141
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:12,900
None of us are crying!

142
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:15,951
What the heck is wrong with these nitwits?

143
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:20,017
Anyway, you get the gist of it, right?

144
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:21,560
We're no longer friends!

145
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:24,014
The Straw Hats are part of my past now!

146
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:25,740
It's so sad!

147
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:28,397
So sad that I'll have to sing about it!

148
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:31,833
This song is called Severed
Friendship, North Wind Child!

149
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:33,540
Yeah! Sing it, bro!

150
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:35,090
Hey!

151
00:11:35,173 --> 00:11:36,289
Are you making fun of me?!

152
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:44,540
Is the tea ready yet?

153
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:47,500
I'm dying of thirst over here. Almost.

154
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:50,156
We'll bring it over in just a moment.

155
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:51,850
Wow.

156
00:11:51,933 --> 00:11:52,992
That crying wore me out.

157
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:54,812
Not that I really cried.

158
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:00,500
Here you go, bro.

159
00:12:00,740 --> 00:12:02,622
Now be careful, it's really hot.

160
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:07,317
Thank you so much.

161
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:09,753
Okay, I understand what's what, brother.

162
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:15,137
She just got through telling you that!

163
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:16,050
Calm down!

164
00:12:16,780 --> 00:12:19,220
Sorry about that, bro. You okay?

165
00:12:19,720 --> 00:12:22,838
Why don't you take a break
and have some tea as well?

166
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:28,500
I can't blame you.

167
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:31,397
You must be holding quite
the grudge against us.

168
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:34,892
You know, since we stole your
200 million berries and all.

169
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:39,025
Especially because it led to such
a big fight with your captain.

170
00:12:39,720 --> 00:12:40,550
Whatever.

171
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:42,080
What happens, happens.

172
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:44,599
No sense holding a grudge about it.

173
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:46,680
Taking the high road.

174
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:48,160
Oh, manly.

175
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:50,820
About that money.

176
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:53,700
I spent all of it!

177
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:55,940
I'm gonna knock you flat.

178
00:12:56,360 --> 00:13:00,597
Now, now, don't get so worked up,
especially after you showed me?

179
00:13:00,680 --> 00:13:02,798
how dignified and forgiving you are.

180
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:04,220
Come on, brother.

181
00:13:04,820 --> 00:13:08,137
Can't we just let bygones
be bygones? I mean,

182
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:12,977
I haven't flipped out on you for destroying
my home and demolition factory, have I?

183
00:13:13,060 --> 00:13:16,540
And on top of that, you guys
annihilated my subordinates.

184
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:18,757
You bastards, how dare you all kill you?

185
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:21,300
Easy, bro, you gotta control that anger.

186
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:23,530
Arrgh!

187
00:13:34,760 --> 00:13:35,590
Yep,

188
00:13:35,673 --> 00:13:37,337
a lot has happened these past few days,

189
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:39,917
but I do think we're better
off putting it all behind us.

190
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,437
So what do you say we just put the
axe in the ground and call it a day?

191
00:13:43,520 --> 00:13:45,520
I think you mean bury the hatchet.

192
00:13:45,620 --> 00:13:48,502
Yeah, I'm pretty sure it's
bury the hatchet, bro.

193
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:50,157
So what's next?

194
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:52,757
What will you do now
that you're on your own?

195
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:55,197
It doesn't seem like
you have anywhere to go.

196
00:13:55,280 --> 00:13:57,697
Tell you what why don't
you let me take care of you

197
00:13:57,780 --> 00:14:01,137
join the Franky family
You seem like a good man,

198
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:02,297
and I can always get it.

199
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:05,260
I have no plans to
become a ship dismantler

200
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:08,177
Even though I'm no longer a Straw Hat.

201
00:14:08,260 --> 00:14:09,789
I am still a pirate got it

202
00:14:11,140 --> 00:14:12,517
You've moved me to song.

203
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:13,953
This is the pirate code

204
00:14:16,460 --> 00:14:19,695
Don't sing it and I still
think you're making fun of me

205
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:22,220
Look, I'm just concerned, that's all.

206
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:24,097
I'm thinking about your future.

207
00:14:24,180 --> 00:14:26,239
My future is none of your business.

208
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:27,930
Besides,

209
00:14:28,013 --> 00:14:30,397
there are other things you should
be concerned with right now,

210
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:32,597
like that huge storm headed this way.

211
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:34,580
True, but we're safe here.

212
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:37,757
Aqua Laguna can be pretty devastating.

213
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:40,369
When it hits, we all
have to pull together.

214
00:14:41,720 --> 00:14:43,837
Starting now until late into the night,

215
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:46,417
houses will be submerged
up to the second floor.

216
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:48,957
If you're stuck in the back
streets or capes during that time,

217
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:51,864
You don't stand a chance,
but this area is safe.

218
00:14:51,980 --> 00:14:54,977
Your ship will be safe, too,
if you want to keep it here.

219
00:14:55,060 --> 00:14:56,560
Oh, thank you very much.

220
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:58,097
I really appreciate it.

221
00:14:58,180 --> 00:15:00,017
Stop acting like we're strangers, moron!

222
00:15:00,100 --> 00:15:02,157
I imagine it must be
tough having to deal with

223
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:04,537
this storm and the chaos
it causes every year.

224
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:06,000
Nah, it's not so bad.

225
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:09,017
We've all gotten used to
the annual evacuation.

226
00:15:09,100 --> 00:15:11,240
The real problem is the water level.

227
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:14,264
It increases little by
little every single year.

228
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:18,617
The back streets you see all
through Water 7 are built on the

229
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:22,406
rooftops of an old town that
existed several hundred years ago.

230
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:25,497
It's said that the shipbuilding
island and the back

231
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:28,077
streets used to be part
of the same piece of land.

232
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:30,866
But now, so much of it
has sunk under the sea.

233
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:33,377
That's why they call it a floating city.

234
00:15:33,460 --> 00:15:36,300
All the sidewalks became
canals and waterways.

235
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:38,937
If you want to be more precise about it,

236
00:15:39,020 --> 00:15:41,557
technically, rather than saying
the water level is increasing,

237
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:43,537
the foundation of the
island is actually sinking.

238
00:15:43,620 --> 00:15:47,680
If it keeps up, the city will
become harder and harder to live in.

239
00:15:48,620 --> 00:15:49,450
I see.

240
00:15:49,533 --> 00:15:53,828
Even this beautiful city of water has
its share of hardships, too, doesn't it?

241
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:55,780
Hmm. But still,

242
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:57,297
if push comes to shove,

243
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:01,097
you can just take the Sea Train
and go live somewhere else, right?

244
00:16:01,180 --> 00:16:02,617
You can say that now.

245
00:16:02,700 --> 00:16:03,840
But not always.

246
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:06,278
It's only been around for ten years.

247
00:16:06,380 --> 00:16:08,797
Before that time, you
were totally out of luck.

248
00:16:08,880 --> 00:16:13,351
Getting across the ocean without it's
no easy task, even if you have a boat.

249
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:14,470
Huh?

250
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:16,420
This island is isolated.

251
00:16:16,660 --> 00:16:19,097
There was never much
traffic to or from it.

252
00:16:19,180 --> 00:16:21,977
The people who lived here carried
with them the looming fear that

253
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:26,197
someday this entire city and everything
around it would sink into the sea.

254
00:16:26,280 --> 00:16:27,560
They became hopeless.

255
00:16:39,180 --> 00:16:42,157
The one thing that finally
took away that fear

256
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:46,477
was the Sea Train the
puffing tom now anyone

257
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:48,597
at any time can travel
across the sea without

258
00:16:48,680 --> 00:16:51,740
a Log Pose this was a
huge step for the island

259
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:57,200
And we owe everything to one man,
a brilliant shipwright named Tom,

260
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:58,971
who created that C-train.

261
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:03,680
He saved us all, and he
gave this island new life.

262
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:12,990
Bro!

263
00:17:17,340 --> 00:17:18,780
Help me!

264
00:17:23,900 --> 00:17:27,820
I'll say hello to your bro for
you when we pay him a visit.

265
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:40,460
We now know the location.

266
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:46,050
Let's go.

267
00:18:10,580 --> 00:18:12,756
Hey, got a question for you, brother.

268
00:18:14,420 --> 00:18:16,297
I'll just get right to the point.

269
00:18:16,380 --> 00:18:19,262
What are you going to do
after you fix that ship?

270
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:21,140
Well, let's see.

271
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:23,477
First, we'll go on a
few adventures together,

272
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:27,266
and eventually I'll sail her back
to our home in the East Blue!

273
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:30,377
I know we didn't sail around
the world or anything,

274
00:18:30,460 --> 00:18:32,917
but the Mary and I made it
all the way to the Grand Line!

275
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,237
I'd call that a successful voyage that
will end with a triumphant return!

276
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:37,877
So with our heads held high...

277
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:38,790
Nope, sorry.

278
00:18:38,873 --> 00:18:39,860
Not gonna happen.

279
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:42,757
You can't go all the way
back to the East Blue.

280
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:44,180
It's too far away.

281
00:18:50,200 --> 00:18:51,240
What do you mean?

282
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:56,597
I took a close look at your ship when
we were bringing it up here earlier.

283
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:58,600
Got to see her from every angle.

284
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:01,158
That ship is at the end of her life.

285
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:03,741
Galilaw's assessment was correct.

286
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:08,199
As an expert in the demolition
business, I suggest you dismantle her.

287
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:09,890
What?

288
00:19:09,973 --> 00:19:10,803
That's ridiculous.

289
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:12,880
I'll even help you do the job.

290
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:14,748
Like I said, I'm an expert.

291
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:16,000
Are you crazy?

292
00:19:16,260 --> 00:19:19,142
What in the hell are you
talking about, you jerk?

293
00:19:19,460 --> 00:19:20,290
Hold on!

294
00:19:20,380 --> 00:19:22,300
Don't come any closer! Stop!

295
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:23,480
I need it!

296
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:25,520
Take it easy.

297
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:28,657
I'm simply offering to help
demolish this ship, that's all.

298
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:29,975
Stop screwing around!

299
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:32,597
I'm not going to let you
anywhere near her, understand?

300
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:33,510
Get lost!

301
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:35,020
The Mary is my ship!

302
00:19:37,180 --> 00:19:38,477
You're wrong. Listen.

303
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:40,840
That old girl's no longer a ship.

304
00:19:42,700 --> 00:19:43,530
Huh?

305
00:19:43,613 --> 00:19:44,937
What'd you say? Earlier,

306
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:49,677
I asked you a question about what you
planned on doing once you fixed that ship.

307
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:50,590
Now,

308
00:19:50,673 --> 00:19:52,157
if you had said that you
were going to cast away

309
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:54,697
your life to the sea and
go down along with her,

310
00:19:54,780 --> 00:19:55,610
then,

311
00:19:55,693 --> 00:19:57,757
well, I wouldn't have had
anything to say about it.

312
00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:58,670
No questions.

313
00:20:00,100 --> 00:20:00,930
But,

314
00:20:01,013 --> 00:20:02,817
since you just told me
that you plan on going

315
00:20:02,900 --> 00:20:05,429
all the way back to
the East Blue with her,

316
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:08,580
well, now I have to stop you, brother.

317
00:20:09,420 --> 00:20:10,250
Yes.

318
00:20:11,540 --> 00:20:13,300
That ship is at her end.

319
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:16,071
She won't even make it to the next island.

320
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:19,600
Listen, a ship is born with one promise.

321
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:22,936
to carry you across the sea
from one shore to another.

322
00:20:23,180 --> 00:20:24,640
That's her sole purpose.

323
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:26,740
The reason for her existence.

324
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:28,360
Which means,

325
00:20:28,680 --> 00:20:32,974
when the time comes that a ship can
no longer carry you across the sea...

326
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:36,620
Hey, stop!

327
00:20:37,220 --> 00:20:38,050
No!

328
00:20:39,160 --> 00:20:43,500
Then that promise has been broken,
and she's no longer a ship!

329
00:20:45,400 --> 00:20:46,230
Bro!

330
00:20:53,160 --> 00:20:54,780
No! Stop it!

331
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:09,590
You bastard!

332
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,760
Keep away from the Mary's!

333
00:21:22,460 --> 00:21:25,120
Oh, son of a bitch!

334
00:21:27,320 --> 00:21:29,500
If you don't understand!

335
00:21:34,780 --> 00:21:39,100
Then, take a look with your own damn eyes!

336
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:19,520
SIGHS

337
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:48,240
Oh, my God.

338
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:50,940
Oh, no.

339
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:31,740
Oh, my God.

340
00:23:52,380 --> 00:23:56,440
Oh, my God.

341
00:24:24,260 --> 00:24:25,380
Sorry, Mary.

342
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:30,250
Don't worry.

343
00:24:31,780 --> 00:24:33,368
I'll fix you up right away.

344
00:24:34,220 --> 00:24:35,100
Every time.

345
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:38,340
As many times as you need.

346
00:24:38,940 --> 00:24:39,940
No matter what.

347
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:42,820
I'll always take care of you.

348
00:24:44,040 --> 00:24:44,870
Always.

349
00:24:50,220 --> 00:24:52,740
Hey, enough already, Long Nose.

350
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:54,877
You saw the bottom of the ship, right?

351
00:24:54,960 --> 00:24:57,077
The keel is broken right down the middle.

352
00:24:57,160 --> 00:24:59,957
The outer panels are slipping
off and the frame is shot.

353
00:25:00,040 --> 00:25:03,297
With a hull like that, the
first wave would rip her apart.

354
00:25:03,380 --> 00:25:07,851
And you think that a complete amateur
like you can just grab a hammer and...

355
00:25:15,480 --> 00:25:17,377
You shut your damn mouth!

356
00:25:17,460 --> 00:25:20,107
I don't want to hear
another word out of you!

357
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:24,580
If you so much as breathe, I'll
kick your teeth and you'll hear me!

358
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:40,950
Beside.

359
00:25:43,740 --> 00:25:44,570
Huh?

360
00:25:45,200 --> 00:25:46,377
What did you say?

361
00:25:46,460 --> 00:25:52,560
In my heart, I knew it all along.

362
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:57,720
I knew the truth!

363
00:25:58,100 --> 00:26:01,260
I knew that the Mary was
at the end of our lives!

364
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:10,260
Deep down, I knew it.

365
00:26:13,260 --> 00:26:15,040
When I heard the assessment,

366
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:20,560
I knew that what I saw on that day
back in Skypiea really happened.

367
00:26:23,100 --> 00:26:30,080
At first, I thought I was dreaming
that there was no way it could be real.

368
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:33,460
On one foggy night,

369
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:36,297
in a forest lit with
nothing but moonlight,

370
00:26:36,380 --> 00:26:39,400
I heard the hammering
strikes of a wooden mallet.

371
00:26:39,980 --> 00:26:44,397
It sounded like it was coming from the
direction of the ship, but at the time!

372
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:47,009
there shouldn't have
been anybody on board.

373
00:26:47,320 --> 00:26:48,580
I crept closer.

374
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:52,075
It confirmed the sound was
indeed coming from the Mary.

375
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:56,517
The fog was thick, but I'm
sure I saw someone there?

376
00:26:56,600 --> 00:26:58,940
striking away with a wooden mallet.

377
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:01,940
I couldn't figure out who it was.

378
00:27:04,660 --> 00:27:06,160
Whoa, check it out!

379
00:27:06,360 --> 00:27:07,717
On the morning of the next day,

380
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:11,400
We awoke to find that the ship
had been haphazardly repaired.

381
00:27:12,240 --> 00:27:15,475
Even the broken mast was back
where it had always been.

382
00:27:15,860 --> 00:27:19,566
But there was something even more
peculiar that was bugging me.

383
00:27:20,040 --> 00:27:22,922
Whoever fixed the Mary
knew how she used to look.

384
00:27:23,340 --> 00:27:25,917
Despite all the
modifications we made to her,

385
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:29,677
this mysterious person had brought
her back to her original shape.

386
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:34,800
She was still in rough condition, but she
was the same old Mary she had been before.

387
00:27:35,740 --> 00:27:38,260
The whole situation remained a mystery,

388
00:27:38,620 --> 00:27:41,738
but for some reason these
words lingered in my heart.

389
00:27:42,060 --> 00:27:44,837
I don't know if it's something
I actually heard someone say,

390
00:27:44,920 --> 00:27:46,980
or maybe just something I imagined.

391
00:27:49,100 --> 00:27:50,080
Don't worry.

392
00:27:53,280 --> 00:27:56,200
I'll carry everyone for
a little bit longer.

393
00:27:58,380 --> 00:28:02,117
I know it sounds ridiculous, but I...

394
00:28:02,200 --> 00:28:06,897
I think it was the
incarnation of the Mary,

395
00:28:06,980 --> 00:28:09,240
and she spoke to me.

396
00:28:13,540 --> 00:28:16,880
She probably already knew
that she was at her limits,

397
00:28:17,640 --> 00:28:20,720
and so she appeared in
that form to tell us.

398
00:28:21,340 --> 00:28:24,660
She wanted to reassure us
that it'd be all right.

399
00:28:25,360 --> 00:28:29,184
That's what I thought of when I
heard her assessment from Galley-La.

400
00:28:32,900 --> 00:28:35,697
You think I'm completely
out of my mind, don't you?

401
00:28:35,780 --> 00:28:38,309
I don't care, you don't
have to believe me.

402
00:28:40,080 --> 00:28:41,100
What is it, bro?

403
00:28:42,320 --> 00:28:44,140
Whether I believe it or not,

404
00:28:44,660 --> 00:28:47,017
you said that whoever it was you saw?

405
00:28:47,100 --> 00:28:50,100
looked like someone holding
a wooden mallet, right?

406
00:28:50,860 --> 00:28:51,690
That's right.

407
00:28:52,040 --> 00:28:53,393
Does it mean something?

408
00:28:54,820 --> 00:28:56,200
Yeah, it sure does.

409
00:28:56,640 --> 00:28:59,340
It means that you saw a Klebautermon.

410
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:01,180
Kle-Kleba-what?

411
00:29:03,460 --> 00:29:04,420
A Klebautermon.

412
00:29:05,180 --> 00:29:08,617
It's an old legend that's passed
down from sailor to sailor.

413
00:29:08,700 --> 00:29:10,876
It's a very particular type of ferry.

414
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:13,824
It only dwells on ships
treated with great care.

415
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:16,416
You could say it's the ship's spirit.

416
00:29:18,260 --> 00:29:22,637
It's said that it carries a wooden mallet
in its hand and wears a sailor's raincoat.

417
00:29:22,720 --> 00:29:24,955
At times when the vessel is in danger,

418
00:29:25,100 --> 00:29:28,197
it'll run around the ship and
find some way to warn everyone.

419
00:29:28,280 --> 00:29:31,237
I've heard that they help
sailors in other ways, too.

420
00:29:31,320 --> 00:29:32,860
But to be honest,

421
00:29:33,080 --> 00:29:37,904
this is the first time I've ever heard
someone say outright that they've seen one.

422
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:42,951
The more care a ship receives, the more
they grow to appreciate the sailors.

423
00:29:43,840 --> 00:29:47,617
This ship must have been determined to
make sure you made it to the next shore,

424
00:29:47,700 --> 00:29:48,582
no matter what.

425
00:29:49,340 --> 00:29:50,700
Just think about it.

426
00:29:50,960 --> 00:29:54,195
This ship had a caring crew
that was so special to her,

427
00:29:54,280 --> 00:29:56,737
she even appeared in
human form to reassure

428
00:29:56,820 --> 00:29:58,337
them that everything
was going to be alright.

429
00:29:58,420 --> 00:30:01,540
I bet you she's been very happy.

430
00:30:06,660 --> 00:30:07,490
Mary.

431
00:30:14,540 --> 00:30:15,497
I see.

432
00:30:15,580 --> 00:30:17,340
So, it was you.

433
00:30:18,060 --> 00:30:22,707
You were the one who made those repairs
and spoke to me back then, weren't you?

434
00:30:24,280 --> 00:30:25,110
Mary.

435
00:30:35,320 --> 00:30:38,300
As amazing as that is, I'm still confused.

436
00:30:39,300 --> 00:30:40,140
Tell me,

437
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:45,244
why would you have a big fight with your
crew when you knew the ship was done for?

438
00:30:47,500 --> 00:30:48,880
It's not that simple.

439
00:30:50,300 --> 00:30:53,400
I'll never understand
why men are so stupid!

440
00:30:54,040 --> 00:30:55,158
You're such idiots!

441
00:31:04,280 --> 00:31:06,277
Sounds like someone's at the door.

442
00:31:06,360 --> 00:31:09,017
Someone who else would it be?

443
00:31:09,100 --> 00:31:10,629
But zombie and the others!

444
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:16,037
Why would they take the long way
and come in from the seaside?

445
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:17,237
Yeah, that's odd.

446
00:31:17,320 --> 00:31:20,137
They normally come in
through the upper entrance.

447
00:31:20,220 --> 00:31:23,867
Maybe they had to because they
were able to capture Straw Hat.

448
00:31:25,080 --> 00:31:26,317
That's right, I forgot.

449
00:31:26,400 --> 00:31:29,057
I kidnapped you to use your
bait to lure out your crew.

450
00:31:29,140 --> 00:31:30,457
They won't come!

451
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:33,011
I'm no longer part of that crew, remember?

452
00:31:33,200 --> 00:31:34,640
That plan won't work

453
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:43,593
Why are they ringing the bell
when they can just come in?

454
00:31:44,140 --> 00:31:45,037
How annoying?

455
00:31:45,120 --> 00:31:47,780
Okay, okay, hold on, I'm coming!

456
00:31:48,960 --> 00:31:50,817
Enough already, we hear you!

457
00:31:50,900 --> 00:31:51,980
Hold on a second!

458
00:31:56,660 --> 00:32:01,484
All right, brother, listen, I think what
you should do is go back to your friends.

459
00:32:03,520 --> 00:32:05,177
It's impossible to do that now.

460
00:32:05,260 --> 00:32:07,537
I challenge the captain
to a duel for starters!

461
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:11,679
And then there's the matter of the
ship that hasn't been settled yet?

462
00:32:12,440 --> 00:32:14,557
What do you mean hasn't been settled?

463
00:32:14,640 --> 00:32:16,457
If your ship can't sail anymore,

464
00:32:16,540 --> 00:32:19,775
then it can't sail anymore
That's the end of it, right?

465
00:32:23,780 --> 00:32:25,780
Just leave her here with me, okay?

466
00:32:25,960 --> 00:32:28,377
I'll take care of her in a
way that you'd approve of.

467
00:32:28,460 --> 00:32:29,342
Problem solved.

468
00:32:29,560 --> 00:32:31,857
I wasn't thinking about
what I was doing earlier.

469
00:32:31,940 --> 00:32:35,037
I tried to dismantle the ship without
considering what would be best for you.

470
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:36,177
It'll be different this time.

471
00:32:36,260 --> 00:32:37,337
This is my problem!

472
00:32:37,420 --> 00:32:38,655
Stay out of it, okay?

473
00:32:38,880 --> 00:32:42,115
Hey, I'm just trying to help
you - Is that you, zombie?

474
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:44,146
Show some gratitude, will ya?

475
00:32:44,660 --> 00:32:45,490
Gratitude?

476
00:32:45,573 --> 00:32:46,987
You've got to be kidding me!

477
00:32:48,660 --> 00:32:50,377
Don't you understand this situation?

478
00:32:50,460 --> 00:32:53,297
As hard as it is for you,
it's worse on the ship.

479
00:32:53,380 --> 00:32:57,017
She loves you guys so much that
she appeared to you in human form.

480
00:32:57,100 --> 00:32:59,877
If she sinks to the bottom of the
sea while you're still on board,

481
00:32:59,960 --> 00:33:01,977
then all of that will
have been for nothing.

482
00:33:02,060 --> 00:33:02,890
Get it?

483
00:33:02,973 --> 00:33:05,837
If that happens, her final moments
will be filled with regret.

484
00:33:05,920 --> 00:33:07,577
She won't be able to die in peace.

485
00:33:07,660 --> 00:33:08,757
Is that what you'd do?

486
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:10,337
After everything you'd
been through together,

487
00:33:10,420 --> 00:33:12,977
you'd leave one of your friends
behind to die on their own?

488
00:33:13,060 --> 00:33:15,001
Just throw them aside like trash?

489
00:33:15,240 --> 00:33:17,137
That's not the same thing at all.

490
00:33:17,220 --> 00:33:18,557
This is completely different.

491
00:33:18,640 --> 00:33:22,040
It is the same thing, and
that's exactly what's going on!

492
00:33:22,220 --> 00:33:24,280
Ugh, you just don't get it!

493
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:30,470
Mozu!

494
00:33:36,560 --> 00:33:37,390
Kiwi!

495
00:33:37,500 --> 00:33:39,480
I apologize for the interruption.

496
00:33:43,240 --> 00:33:48,060
It's just those two ladies weren't being
very accommodating about letting us in.

497
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:52,020
What the hell are you
bastards doing down here?

498
00:33:55,280 --> 00:33:56,360
Iron body.

499
00:33:56,500 --> 00:33:58,680
Hey, what's the big idea?

500
00:34:02,220 --> 00:34:03,600
What gives, Belt?

501
00:34:03,940 --> 00:34:06,116
You got some kind of problem with me?

502
00:34:06,300 --> 00:34:08,712
We're trying to pull a reverse move, huh?

503
00:34:08,940 --> 00:34:11,646
You dumbass, you should
know better than that.

504
00:34:13,640 --> 00:34:15,720
No one pushes me around.

505
00:34:20,260 --> 00:34:22,789
You should be pouring
me a drink, remember?

506
00:34:23,500 --> 00:34:25,912
What, you forget you're just a bartender?

507
00:34:26,020 --> 00:34:28,237
And what's up with that outfit anyway?

508
00:34:28,320 --> 00:34:29,967
What the hell are you up to?

509
00:34:33,020 --> 00:34:33,880
Not yet.

510
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:43,860
Whoa, are you okay?

511
00:34:44,780 --> 00:34:47,197
Killing him now would put
our mission in jeopardy.

512
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:48,339
We need him alive.

513
00:34:48,920 --> 00:34:50,940
Hey, what's going on here?

514
00:34:51,120 --> 00:34:54,557
I mean, what did you do to piss
off these shipyard guys so bad?

515
00:34:54,640 --> 00:34:55,857
The freak with the bird?

516
00:34:55,940 --> 00:34:57,717
How'd he get a hit like that on me?

517
00:34:57,800 --> 00:34:58,630
And what's the deal?

518
00:34:58,713 --> 00:35:00,737
Why are you guys coming
in on my turf with a chip

519
00:35:00,820 --> 00:35:02,997
on your shoulders acting
like you own the place?

520
00:35:03,080 --> 00:35:04,139
And hitting girls?

521
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:05,560
That's so not cool!

522
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:08,271
Kiwi and Mozu were like my little sisters!

523
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:11,459
You know, how'd you freaks
even find us here anyway?

524
00:35:11,620 --> 00:35:13,740
This is my ultra top secret base!

525
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:16,897
How we found you is irrelevant,
as we are here right now.

526
00:35:16,980 --> 00:35:20,737
But know this we came to this town
assigned to a covert operation

527
00:35:20,820 --> 00:35:24,537
with special agents spies
working for the world government.

528
00:35:24,620 --> 00:35:29,540
I Assume even you would know why
we came here and what we're after?

529
00:35:30,980 --> 00:35:34,120
Franky we know the secret
that you're keeping.

530
00:35:34,740 --> 00:35:35,880
Cutty Flam

531
00:35:36,580 --> 00:35:40,680
The rumor was that Tom's apprentice
perished about eight years ago.

532
00:35:41,300 --> 00:35:43,080
But you're still alive.

533
00:35:45,140 --> 00:35:50,157
That's some pretty amazing detective work
i'm real impressed but i'm starting to

534
00:35:50,240 --> 00:35:53,717
get a sour feeling down in my gut where

535
00:35:53,800 --> 00:35:57,460
is he where the hell is iceberg he's dead.

536
00:36:00,100 --> 00:36:04,500
It's too late to feign innocence
we already know everything.

537
00:36:06,820 --> 00:36:09,777
It's only a matter of time
before the government targets me.

538
00:36:09,860 --> 00:36:13,660
I want you to take these and
leave this island, Franky.

539
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:18,840
Tom entrusted them to Iceberg,
and Iceberg entrusted them to you.

540
00:36:20,540 --> 00:36:22,180
Hey, boys, come here.

541
00:36:22,500 --> 00:36:25,357
It's about time I let you
in on something important.

542
00:36:25,440 --> 00:36:28,760
It's been a long game,
but now we're a checkmate.

543
00:36:29,420 --> 00:36:32,680
See, I know you have the
plans for the ancient weapon.

544
00:36:33,060 --> 00:36:36,080
Hand over Pluton's blueprints, Kaido Flam.

545
00:36:37,060 --> 00:36:37,940
Flam, huh?

546
00:36:38,220 --> 00:36:39,777
That's kind of a funny name.

547
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:42,389
How about we just call you Franky instead?

548
00:36:42,600 --> 00:36:43,430
Whatever.

549
00:36:43,920 --> 00:36:45,240
Are you listening?

550
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:48,960
Hand them over this instant.

551
00:36:50,260 --> 00:36:52,420
Oh, did you just say hand?

552
00:37:04,860 --> 00:37:07,040
What the hell did he just do?

553
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:11,800
It was so fast I couldn't see
what happened. Hey! Are you okay?

554
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:18,940
Oh, my.

555
00:37:19,600 --> 00:37:21,300
You found a room.

556
00:37:21,640 --> 00:37:24,700
What do you suppose is this
filthy place's purpose?

557
00:37:25,020 --> 00:37:26,549
Those are drafting tables.

558
00:37:26,800 --> 00:37:30,380
Yes, where blueprints are
made, and perhaps hidden.

559
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:31,920
Find them.

560
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:51,060
Look here, nameplates, Kaido Flam,
Iceberg, Tom, the Shipwright.

561
00:37:53,460 --> 00:37:54,920
This picture.

562
00:37:56,340 --> 00:37:58,620
Keep your paws off of that.

563
00:38:00,560 --> 00:38:05,420
You're poking your noses into
my personal space, my memories.

564
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:08,917
This is the place where I grew up.

565
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:12,820
What used to house the shipbuilding
company, Tom's Workers?
